{
  "id": "zero_sum.dantzig_proof_spine",
  "title": "Dantzig Proof Spine for Minimax (Ordered-Field Route)",
  "kind": "proof-plan",
  "status": "admitted",
  "href": "zero_sum/zero_sum_dantzig_proof_spine.html",
  "primary_topic": "zero_sum",
  "topics": [
    "zero_sum",
    "zero_sum.minimax",
    "zero_sum.lp"
  ],
  "body_html": "<h1>Dantzig Proof Spine for Minimax (Ordered-Field Route)</h1>\n<p>This proof-plan node records the <strong>alternative</strong>, ordered-field-generic route\nto the von Neumann minimax theorem. The primary route used by the library is\nthe simplified Loomis induction over $\\mathbb{R}$\n(<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"zero_sum.minimax.minimax_from_loomis\" href=\"minimax/zero_sum_minimax_minimax_from_loomis.html\">Minimax via the All-Ones Specialization of Loomis</a>). The Dantzig route is retained\nbecause:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It works over any linearly ordered field, not just $\\mathbb{R}$ — a strict\n   generalisation of Loomis.</li>\n<li>It yields a <em>constructive</em> simplex algorithm in Lean, useful as a\n   future basis for executable extraction.</li>\n<li>Most of its algebraic scaffolding is already proved; only the\n   simplex-termination step (the top-level <code>dantzig_minimax</code> induction) is\n   open — tracked as the open issue\n   <a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"zero_sum.simplex_termination\" href=\"zero_sum_simplex_termination.html\">Dantzig Simplex Termination (Open)</a> in <code>staged/</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Open.</strong> This route is <em>not yet complete</em>. The lone remaining gap is the\nsimplex algorithm's finite-termination argument; see the proof-plan node\nlinked above for the precise statement and intended approach.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Dantzig's proof represents the minimax theorem through an augmented linear\nsystem.  A basis state stores rows of the inverse basis matrix, a lexicographic\ncondition prevents cycling, and optimality inequalities encode the\ngame-theoretic guarantees.</p>\n<p>At an optimal basis, the extracted row strategy is nonnegative and satisfies\nthe row player's guarantee inequalities.  The same basis data extracts a column\nstrategy for player II.  The pivot step updates the inverse basis while\npreserving the lexicographic condition and strictly decreasing the leading row\nin lexicographic order.</p>",
  "proof_html": "<p>The route starts from Dantzig's augmented system for the shifted\npositive payoff matrix. A basis state contains enough inverse-basis data to\nread off candidate row and column variables. If the current basis is not\noptimal, the pivot rule selects an entering and leaving variable so that\nfeasibility is preserved and the lexicographic leading row strictly decreases.\nThe lexicographic condition rules out cycles, so repeated pivots terminate. At\nthe terminal basis, the optimality inequalities say exactly that the extracted\nrow strategy guarantees the terminal value and the extracted column strategy\ncaps the same value. Normalizing the extracted nonnegative variables gives\nmixed strategies, and undoing the initial constant shift gives the minimax\nequality for the original game.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[Dantzig, Sections 2-3] Dantzig, <em>A Constructive Proof of the Min-Max Theorem</em>, <code>docs/dev/references/Dantzig, CONSTRUCTIVE PROOF OF THE MIN-MAX THEOREM.pdf</code>. Constructive proof of the minimax theorem via simplex-style pivots.</li>\n</ul>",
